`



I have a multi stepper form in RHF that has 10-15 fields for each step. Now I want to build a undo/redo functionality for the form's editability.

That’s a fantastic enhancement—especially for complex multi-step forms where users might want to backtrack without losing progress. React Hook Form (RHF) doesn’t have built-in undo/redo, but you can absolutely implement it with a bit of state history management. Here are two solid approaches:

🧠 Option 1: Use a State History Stack (Manual Approach)
You can track form state snapshots using useRef or useState:

🔧 How it works:
On every field change or step transition, push the current form state into a history array.

Maintain a pointer (currentIndex) to track where you are in the history.

Undo = move pointer back and reset() the form with that snapshot.

Redo = move pointer forward and reset() again.

🧪 Example Skeleton:
tsx
const { control, watch, reset } = useForm();
const [history, setHistory] = useState([]);
const [index, setIndex] = useState(-1);

useEffect(() => {
  const subscription = watch((_, { values }) => {
    setHistory((prev) => [...prev.slice(0, index + 1), values]);
    setIndex((i) => i + 1);
  });
  return () => subscription.unsubscribe();
}, [watch]);

const undo = () => {
  if (index > 0) {
    setIndex(index - 1);
    reset(history[index - 1]);
  }
};

const redo = () => {
  if (index < history.length - 1) {
    setIndex(index + 1);
    reset(history[index + 1]);
  }
};
🧬 Option 2: Use Jotai + jotai-history (Elegant + Scalable)
If you're already using Jotai or open to it, this is a clean and reactive solution. One open-source project demonstrates this beautifully by syncing RHF with Jotai atoms and using jotai-history for undo/redo.

✨ Benefits:
Bidirectional sync between RHF and Jotai

Undo/redo with time-travel-like precision

Clean separation of form state and UI

You can check out the RHF + Jotai sync demo for inspiration.

🧩 Bonus Tips for Multi-Step Forms
Track history per step if you want granular control.

Use useFormContext() if your steps are split into separate components.

Consider debouncing the history updates to avoid flooding the stack on every keystroke.

Would you like me to help you scaffold a reusable useFormHistory() hook that works with RHF out of the box? It could make your undo/redo logic plug-and-play.





`

